#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593 :topic/id #uuid "ac718bb5-4681-5ba0-883a-072801cad492" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054455 :question/id #uuid "67ce214a-e9e9-5a32-b48c-d9488cb141ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-134" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ when $f(x)=x^3$." :question/worked-solution "The power rule states that if\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^n\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf'(x) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^3\n$$\n\nwe have $n=3$. So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 3 \\cdot x^{3 - 1} = 3x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32dd6880-3725-564a-9e85-0d39b6960c72" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Power Function Using the Power Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054456 :question/id #uuid "54c18c81-9deb-5958-81a0-1abecf91ba58" :question/math-academy-id "e-135" :question/problem "Calculate the derivative of $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we can rewrite the function as\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{1}{x^2} = x^{-2}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with $n=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (-2) \\cdot x^{-2 - 1} \\\\\n&=-2x^{-3} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2ff1808-d239-5625-93b7-21a98ada3fab" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Negative Power Function Using the Power Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054812 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054457 :question/id #uuid "1717a513-6866-5b4e-8c9a-1ea7f343acab" :question/math-academy-id "e-136" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ when $y=\\sqrt{x}$." :question/worked-solution "Writing the radical as a rational exponent, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}=x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with\n\n$$\nn=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{1/2 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d06cd8-9aae-50ab-90dc-6c70b545d569" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Fractional Power Function Using the Power Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054465 :question/id #uuid "27baaab1-2088-5277-be24-5dfba2bfa5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-137" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 12$." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is constant, its derivative is zero. Therefore, $f'(x)=0$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "654f8868-880b-53dc-ba4c-677aecb10b95" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Constant Function Using the Power Rule"}] :topic/math-academy-id 35 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049481}] :topic/title "The Power Rule for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420 :topic/difficulty 1.0 :topic/id #uuid "476d32c8-5b16-54ef-9688-daca6c7937ad" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059503 :question/id #uuid "31b9e2d8-1458-50b3-a920-703476825420" :question/math-academy-id "e-3631" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y =  3\\cos x  + 4\\sin x + 2x^3.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\sin x ) = \\cos x,$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\cos x ) = -\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3cos⁡x+4sin⁡x+2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+2(3){x}^{2} \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0de0627b-2568-5c02-8928-d14344df5a2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059676 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059504 :question/id #uuid "ae922c00-2ff5-5a46-805a-62955bc5a08a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3632" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y = 4\\tan x + 5x$ at $x = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope at $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative $y'$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (\\tan x) = \\sec^2 x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4tan⁡x+5x) \\\\  & =4{sec}^{2}⁡x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =4{sec}^{2}⁡0+5 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $9.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "711f198b-26b4-5185-812a-102329d49313" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 280 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902}] :topic/title "Differentiating Trigonometric Functions"}]}
